WEBVTT

00:00:01.410 --> 00:00:02.730
GUUST YSEBIE: Hello, LM.

00:00:02.730 --> 00:00:05.850
So today we're going
to talk a little bit

00:00:05.850 --> 00:00:07.290
about designing systems

00:00:07.290 --> 00:00:10.410
for every user including
people and LLMs.

00:00:10.410 --> 00:00:12.840
My name is Guust Ysebie
and I'm currently working

00:00:12.840 --> 00:00:16.410
as a software engineer
enterprise where I mostly work

00:00:16.410 --> 00:00:18.540
on our PDF as the gate.

00:00:18.540 --> 00:00:22.440
So I also work a lot on
accessibility within PDFs

00:00:22.440 --> 00:00:27.210
and that's the knowledge I
want to share with you today.

00:00:27.210 --> 00:00:32.310
PDF documents today are not
only consumed by people.

00:00:32.310 --> 00:00:35.160
They are also being
consumed by LLMs.

00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:38.370
So they should be as
accessible as possible

00:00:38.370 --> 00:00:41.700
for both these consumers.

00:00:41.700 --> 00:00:45.300
Which principles are
useful for everyone?

00:00:45.300 --> 00:00:47.040
The first one is perceivable.

00:00:47.040 --> 00:00:48.420
It needs to be operable.

00:00:48.420 --> 00:00:50.160
It needs to be understandable.

00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:51.750
And it needs to be robust.

00:00:51.750 --> 00:00:53.400
Why am I telling
you all of this?

00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:57.270
It's because it's all of those
kind of issues that both humans

00:00:57.270 --> 00:01:01.110
and LLMs encounter when
working with PDF documents.

00:01:01.110 --> 00:01:04.230
Let's have a quick
look at the PDF.

00:01:04.230 --> 00:01:06.450
When you have physical
data format it means

00:01:06.450 --> 00:01:10.620
that you can use different
kinds of formats like fonts,

00:01:10.620 --> 00:01:14.370
like images, videos, put
them together in to one.

00:01:14.370 --> 00:01:16.650
The most important
parts you'll have

00:01:16.650 --> 00:01:19.830
to understand is the
drawing language,

00:01:19.830 --> 00:01:23.370
and the drawing language is
just drawing instructions

00:01:23.370 --> 00:01:25.260
which you can see displayed.

00:01:25.260 --> 00:01:27.960
What this does is when
you have a canvas

00:01:27.960 --> 00:01:32.250
like a PDF page it
just simply says like,

00:01:32.250 --> 00:01:35.550
"Move to this certain
location and then we need

00:01:35.550 --> 00:01:39.150
to draw these characters
on this exact location."

00:01:39.150 --> 00:01:43.410
This ensures that when you
open the PDF I created

00:01:43.410 --> 00:01:47.550
that your PDF knows
exactly on which pixel

00:01:47.550 --> 00:01:49.260
to render which content.

00:01:49.260 --> 00:01:53.190
The pixels and characters
are like very different

00:01:53.190 --> 00:01:58.110
from what we actually do
when we look at the PDF.

00:01:58.110 --> 00:02:02.220
Those pixels we can actually
design a reading order to it.

00:02:02.220 --> 00:02:06.090
We understand its semantics
then and based on drawing

00:02:06.090 --> 00:02:09.600
of the (inaudible) like
lines, like list symbols,

00:02:09.600 --> 00:02:14.220
we can determine what the order
(inaudible) the data to be in.

00:02:14.220 --> 00:02:17.400
Of course if you're an LLM
you don't have all that kind

00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:19.860
of meta information
out of the box.

00:02:19.860 --> 00:02:23.610
The LLM just looks at the
page and sees pixels.

00:02:23.610 --> 00:02:28.140
So it's very hard for LLMs then
to extract the data from it.

00:02:28.140 --> 00:02:31.410
You would lose either a lot
of tokens where you need

00:02:31.410 --> 00:02:37.200
to have training or you would
need those text extraction

00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:40.350
so you can actually first
extract the data from it

00:02:40.350 --> 00:02:44.010
and then train your
LLM on the data.

00:02:44.010 --> 00:02:50.280
So what you have to remember
here is what we see is very easy

00:02:50.280 --> 00:02:54.990
for us because we are trained
on it, but LLMs don't have this

00:02:54.990 --> 00:02:56.850
and so it's very hard for them

00:02:56.850 --> 00:03:00.390
to interpret the actual
meaning behind those pixels.

00:03:00.390 --> 00:03:03.210
If you, for example,
would have a PDF,

00:03:03.210 --> 00:03:07.320
LLMs actually are quite good in
extracting this kind of data.

00:03:07.320 --> 00:03:09.660
But, as you can see
on the left side,

00:03:09.660 --> 00:03:11.160
it doesn't have any structure.

00:03:11.160 --> 00:03:15.060
It doesn't really
know what means what.

00:03:15.060 --> 00:03:16.200
Yeah. We can make a guess

00:03:16.200 --> 00:03:19.530
and you will input
probably as a title.

00:03:19.530 --> 00:03:24.600
But it's very hard and very
inefficient to train your LLMs

00:03:24.600 --> 00:03:27.870
on because it can't
apply semantic reasoning

00:03:27.870 --> 00:03:29.640
on to the context.

00:03:29.640 --> 00:03:32.910
On the right side, on the other
hand, exactly the same context,

00:03:32.910 --> 00:03:35.130
but now we added
semantic meaning.

00:03:35.130 --> 00:03:39.420
We added which part of the
document is actually a header,

00:03:39.420 --> 00:03:43.290
which one is a table, and as
you can see it will be far more

00:03:43.290 --> 00:03:47.610
easier for the LLM and
for your normal users

00:03:47.610 --> 00:03:51.000
to actually extract
the semantic meaning.

00:03:51.000 --> 00:03:56.280
So in PDFs, normal PDFs, didn't
really have like this kind

00:03:56.280 --> 00:04:01.770
of system where you can take
contents with semantic meaning.

00:04:01.770 --> 00:04:04.200
But of course PDF evolved

00:04:04.200 --> 00:04:06.870
and now it includes a
mechanism for this.

00:04:06.870 --> 00:04:09.270
This mechanism is text PDFs.

00:04:09.270 --> 00:04:12.990
You as a developer
have the advantages

00:04:12.990 --> 00:04:15.300
because low level
tools do it for you

00:04:15.300 --> 00:04:19.620
because if we open the PDF
now, for example here,

00:04:19.620 --> 00:04:23.010
we can quickly see that now

00:04:23.010 --> 00:04:25.200
on the right we have
our text structure.

00:04:25.200 --> 00:04:27.030
So it means we
actually have kind

00:04:27.030 --> 00:04:28.950
of the same structure
you would know

00:04:28.950 --> 00:04:33.000
from HTML while still having
pixel perfect rendering

00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:33.840
on the left.

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:37.620
So now we for sure know
that for example the text

00:04:37.620 --> 00:04:39.720
in blue is in H1.

00:04:39.720 --> 00:04:41.520
Below it is a paragraph.

00:04:41.520 --> 00:04:42.990
And then we have a table.

00:04:42.990 --> 00:04:46.110
So now we have pixel
perfect rendering

00:04:46.110 --> 00:04:49.350
and we have complete
semantic information

00:04:49.350 --> 00:04:52.320
and meta data about
our document.

00:04:52.320 --> 00:04:56.580
So because we now have like this
bunch of semantic information,

00:04:56.580 --> 00:04:59.370
bunch of meta data, and
creating tools on top

00:04:59.370 --> 00:05:01.890
of PDFs are far more easy.

00:05:01.890 --> 00:05:04.440
Now we can just look in
to our text structure

00:05:04.440 --> 00:05:07.020
and see which text is there.

00:05:07.020 --> 00:05:10.470
This means assistive
technology, search, extraction,

00:05:10.470 --> 00:05:14.190
and all the other things are
far more easier to implement

00:05:14.190 --> 00:05:19.110
because now we have a
structured model of the data

00:05:19.110 --> 00:05:22.200
that we display within
our PDF document.

00:05:22.200 --> 00:05:26.370
Text extraction is probably one
of the most interesting things

00:05:26.370 --> 00:05:28.110
because lots of developers need

00:05:28.110 --> 00:05:31.470
to process a bunch
of PDF documents.

00:05:31.470 --> 00:05:34.860
So for our table today
I did a quick demo

00:05:34.860 --> 00:05:37.740
where I built an
LLM data pipeline

00:05:37.740 --> 00:05:39.510
and on the first
(inaudible) we're going

00:05:39.510 --> 00:05:43.320
to use OCR based solution.

00:05:43.320 --> 00:05:46.950
We are going to use Docling
in our circumstance.

00:05:46.950 --> 00:05:49.950
Why? Because it's the
most well used and one

00:05:49.950 --> 00:05:52.410
of the best tools out there.

00:05:52.410 --> 00:05:54.750
It's open source as well.

00:05:54.750 --> 00:05:58.800
And on the other hand we are
going to use iText as the gate

00:05:58.800 --> 00:06:02.940
to leverage the embedded
text system within the PDF

00:06:02.940 --> 00:06:04.950
which contains all
the meta data.

00:06:04.950 --> 00:06:07.380
Instead of trying to
analyze pictures,

00:06:07.380 --> 00:06:10.770
to actually extract the
content we really want to.

00:06:10.770 --> 00:06:12.780
So let's get started.

00:06:12.780 --> 00:06:15.330
So we have this PDF document.

00:06:15.330 --> 00:06:20.490
This is just a normal summary
with some tables, some lists,

00:06:20.490 --> 00:06:26.220
and some stress tests where we
have, for example, a water mark

00:06:26.220 --> 00:06:29.880
in to it and some very
tiny little text.

00:06:29.880 --> 00:06:31.620
So the first thing we're going

00:06:31.620 --> 00:06:34.200
to do is we're going
to run Docling on it.

00:06:34.200 --> 00:06:36.750
And we're going to convert
it to a mark down file.

00:06:36.750 --> 00:06:38.130
Why mark down file?

00:06:38.130 --> 00:06:41.790
Mark down files are
very information dense

00:06:41.790 --> 00:06:46.080
so it means you have
semantic information.

00:06:46.080 --> 00:06:48.060
So let's run Docling first.

00:06:48.060 --> 00:06:55.740
This script is quite easy and
can just be executed with item.

00:06:55.740 --> 00:06:59.010
This just reads the PDF document

00:06:59.010 --> 00:07:01.380
and calls the correct
library functions

00:07:01.380 --> 00:07:03.900
to convert it to a mark down.

00:07:03.900 --> 00:07:07.800
While this is running and
trying to extract the data

00:07:07.800 --> 00:07:11.490
if we have a look at this
this is still warming up.

00:07:11.490 --> 00:07:14.100
It's loading a bunch
of its models.

00:07:14.100 --> 00:07:16.950
This only has to be done
once, of course, for Docling.

00:07:16.950 --> 00:07:22.120
And so now it will start
doing the actual conversion.

00:07:24.960 --> 00:07:28.570
As you see, it takes
a bit of time.

00:07:30.900 --> 00:07:33.630
So now you can see
it's finally done.

00:07:33.630 --> 00:07:39.150
So to convert those four
pages of information based

00:07:39.150 --> 00:07:41.310
on processing the OCR

00:07:41.310 --> 00:07:46.080
so processing the pixels
it takes about 18 seconds.

00:07:46.080 --> 00:07:49.920
So if we look at the
output we generated

00:07:49.920 --> 00:07:53.490
from this we see it's
actually quite good.

00:07:53.490 --> 00:07:55.020
We have all the tables.

00:07:55.020 --> 00:07:57.300
We have the lists.

00:07:57.300 --> 00:08:00.630
We have even the
difficult languages.

00:08:00.630 --> 00:08:03.000
But we also have the
water mark, for example,

00:08:03.000 --> 00:08:05.730
which is not something
we really want.

00:08:05.730 --> 00:08:09.330
So it's not the author intent
to be actually extracted.

00:08:09.330 --> 00:08:14.580
And this might be used if
you're using OCR tools

00:08:14.580 --> 00:08:16.080
to (inaudible) training data

00:08:16.080 --> 00:08:20.100
because then the data will also
be in to your mark down file

00:08:20.100 --> 00:08:25.200
and it might be used or
abused to jailbreak your LLM.

00:08:25.200 --> 00:08:29.430
So how does it compare to the
basic Java implementation

00:08:29.430 --> 00:08:32.940
where we use iText to
actually extract the data

00:08:32.940 --> 00:08:34.680
and the meta
information from it?

00:08:34.680 --> 00:08:37.170
Now it's a far different story.

00:08:37.170 --> 00:08:42.630
So now you see it's
only 0.75 seconds.

00:08:42.630 --> 00:08:43.230
Excuse me.

00:08:43.230 --> 00:08:45.930
0.075 seconds.

00:08:45.930 --> 00:08:50.190
So it's 200 times faster
than the OCR implementation.

00:08:50.190 --> 00:08:52.080
And not only that.

00:08:52.080 --> 00:08:57.930
If we look at the output you
see the tables look exactly

00:08:57.930 --> 00:08:58.440
the same.

00:08:58.440 --> 00:09:00.720
The content looks
exactly the same.

00:09:00.720 --> 00:09:05.250
But, for example, the water mark
which the author didn't intend

00:09:05.250 --> 00:09:09.480
to be actually
displayed or to be used

00:09:09.480 --> 00:09:14.790
or to be actual important data
is not there as we expected,

00:09:14.790 --> 00:09:19.050
and it's of course a lot better
for security reasons and all

00:09:19.050 --> 00:09:20.790
of that, all those things.

00:09:20.790 --> 00:09:23.400
But what you also have
to take in to account,

00:09:23.400 --> 00:09:30.540
if we compare the list of which
it notes we see here PDF OCR

00:09:30.540 --> 00:09:32.730
didn't manage to
actually extract

00:09:32.730 --> 00:09:34.590
that those were sublists.

00:09:34.590 --> 00:09:38.190
Why? Again because the
semantic meaning was lost

00:09:38.190 --> 00:09:43.410
from the document while if
you are using the meta data

00:09:43.410 --> 00:09:47.250
which was embedded in to the
document you see the sublist is

00:09:47.250 --> 00:09:49.230
correctly constructed.

00:09:49.230 --> 00:09:52.680
This means that the Docling
OCR actually generates

00:09:52.680 --> 00:09:55.950
like some kind of mistakes
which are very hard to trace

00:09:55.950 --> 00:09:59.010
and those mistakes can
compound over time

00:09:59.010 --> 00:10:03.180
which in the end produces
very wrong output formats.

00:10:03.180 --> 00:10:04.470
So that was the table.

00:10:04.470 --> 00:10:08.760
As you can see, we generate
the same mark down documents

00:10:08.760 --> 00:10:10.980
to then train our LLMs models

00:10:10.980 --> 00:10:15.000
to extract the business
data that we require.

00:10:15.000 --> 00:10:17.370
So what do we have
to remember here?

00:10:17.370 --> 00:10:18.510
We need to design

00:10:18.510 --> 00:10:20.670
for understanding
not just rendering,

00:10:20.670 --> 00:10:23.220
and that's especially the
case in PDF documents.

00:10:23.220 --> 00:10:28.850
So when you are producing a
PDF you should enable that,

00:10:28.850 --> 00:10:31.760
the meta data, as
being embedded.

00:10:31.760 --> 00:10:37.200
This can be done in most PDF
libraries by enabling a flag.

00:10:37.200 --> 00:10:41.580
Then you have to make sure
that those tags are correct

00:10:41.580 --> 00:10:45.150
and convey the author's
intent of the PDF document.

00:10:45.150 --> 00:10:48.570
And if you do those two
steps you'll have a bunch

00:10:48.570 --> 00:10:50.940
of advantages you get for free.

00:10:50.940 --> 00:10:52.890
You get smart data extraction.

00:10:52.890 --> 00:10:54.540
You get easy search.

00:10:54.540 --> 00:10:58.920
And your PDF documents
are accessible.

00:10:58.920 --> 00:11:03.000
So you have to remember
accessibility isn't extra work.

00:11:03.000 --> 00:11:07.500
What you do now is only a
few more minutes of work

00:11:07.500 --> 00:11:10.590
and that means that
your AI infrastructure

00:11:10.590 --> 00:11:14.280
which you will build in the
next following years will be

00:11:14.280 --> 00:11:18.690
so much better at processing
all your documents

00:11:18.690 --> 00:11:23.130
which means you will have
gained a competitive edge just

00:11:23.130 --> 00:11:27.090
because you make
your data accessible

00:11:27.090 --> 00:11:30.240
to both users and LLMs.

00:11:30.240 --> 00:11:31.020
All right.

00:11:31.020 --> 00:11:32.610
Thank you very much.

00:11:32.610 --> 00:11:35.580
If there are any questions
please don't hesitate

00:11:35.580 --> 00:11:41.200
to contact me or enterprise and
we will gladly help you out.
